msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:349
#: 8b04aafb2053498da54d35649dde6dbb
msgid "How to re-verify installation media after writing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:351
#: f2cedc2100ef42129264084e32839cec
msgid "This is an optional section intended for advanced users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:353
#: c4fc2f88fda1493996cea83270552619
msgid ""
"**Why would you want to re-verify the data that has been written when you’ve "
"already verified the original ISO?** Well, it’s conceivable that a "
"sufficiently sophisticated adversary might allow your initial ISO "
"verification to succeed (so as not to alert you that your machine has been "
"compromised, for example), then surreptitiously modify the data as it is "
"being written onto your installation medium, resulting in a compromised "
"Qubes installer. This might increase the odds that the attack goes "
"undetected. One way to mitigate this risk is to re-verify the installer "
"after writing it onto an installation medium that cannot be altered, such as "
"a USB drive with a properly implemented physical write-protect switch and "
"firmware that is either unflashable or cryptographically signed (or both), "
"as discussed in our :doc:`installation security considerations </user/"
"downloading-installing-upgrading/install-security>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:355
#: ea46c0327150484dbe2dbf7c580079a0
msgid "Before we proceed, you must have:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:357
#: 41814aa97fc64148a8ab5fbfb9e37005
msgid ":ref:`an authentic RSK <how-to-import-RSK>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:358
#: 9c675e630ec5446a86012626938411ce
msgid ""
":ref:`written a Qubes ISO onto your desired medium <user/downloading-"
"installing-upgrading/installation-guide:copying the iso onto the "
"installation medium>` (such as a USB drive or optical disc)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:360
#: f1de4a6baaa34182b445cec1cd07b9a5
msgid ""
"**First**, unplug your USB drive and flip the write protect switch so that "
"the data on the drive can no longer be altered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:362
#: 870a02d2777d460db71ffd6e892a5d60
msgid "Then, you have two options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:364
#: 9ac9d2db8e7144e0a9ea4c626fb589cf
msgid ""
"**If you have a different computer** from the one you used to create the "
"installation medium, consider using that computer, preferably an offline "
"computer that has never seen the original ISO."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:365
#: 5837284a67b84cbcb9926aa2a3587545
msgid ""
"**If not**, try to at least use a fresh VM (e.g., a new qube if it’s a Qubes "
"system), preferably offline and with a storage space that is too small to "
"hold the original ISO"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:367
#: b3b59e12ff1145678610900056e27990
msgid ""
"The idea is that the original system may have been compromised, and using a "
"different one for re-verification forces your hypothetical adversary to "
"compromise an additional system in order to succeed. After all, if your "
"adversary knows the answer you’re looking for — namely, a match to the "
"genuine ISO — and has access to that very ISO in the same re-verification "
"environment, then there is little to prevent them from simply reading the "
"original ISO and feeding you that result (perhaps while also reading from "
"the USB drive and piping it into ``/dev/null`` so that you see the light on "
"the USB drive blinking to support the illusion that the data is being read "
"from the USB drive)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:369
#: 58c475a7044f42ea9ecbb3dd6d085776
msgid ""
"Now, our goal is to perform **the same verification steps as we did with the "
"original ISO**, except, this time, we’ll be reading the installer data "
"directly from the write-protected USB drive instead of from the original ISO "
"file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:371
#: 8ee7c9294c8848a58d2982d53280f96b
msgid ""
"In order to do this, **we have to know the exact size, in bytes, of the "
"original ISO**. There are two ways to get this information:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:373
#: 1424aa206006495b8b212021b1b8b60f
msgid ""
"**from the Qubes website**, by hovering over any ISO download button on the "
"`downloads page <https://www.qubes-os.org/downloads/>`__. You can also view "
"these values directly in the `downloads page’s source data <https://github."
"com/QubesOS/qubesos.github.io/blob/master/_data/downloads.yml>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:374
#: 1c69a1459f3749d488e6a04b7a383da8
msgid "**from the ISO itself**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:381
#: 23f4f717614347a8935cbcdf2bde963a
msgid ""
"Where :file:`Qubes-R{X}-x86_64.iso` is your Qubes ISO (with :samp:`{X}` "
"replaced by the release number). Note that your actual byte number will "
"depend on which Qubes ISO you’re using. This is just an example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:383
#: d831eeb0a33c444aa550ef6965b75047
msgid ""
"You can now use :program:`gpg` to verify the detached PGP signature directly "
"against the data on the USB drive. The following command reads the exact "
"number of bytes from your USB drive and pipes them into :program:`gpg`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:398
#: 09290176fee8427386c36cd2c4fc9536
msgid ""
"Where :file:`/dev/{sdX}` is your USB drive, :samp:`<SIZE>` is the exact size "
"in bytes of the original ISO and ``Qubes-RX-x86_64.iso.asc`` is the detached "
"PGP signature file of the original ISO."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:400
#: 47ba7015856047139c40cb894f9dcc62
msgid ""
"The usual form of a :program:`gpg` verification command is ``gpg --verify "
"<SIGNATURE> <SIGNED_DATA>``. Our command is using shell redirection in order "
"to use data from your USB drive as the ``<SIGNED_DATA>``, which is why the "
"``-`` at the end of the command is required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:403
#: b86d2a56d8f149e79c54e233d2f6a0c1
msgid ""
"**The output you receive will not look exactly the same**, what matters is "
"that you should receive a ``Good signature`` message for the :ref:"
"`appropriate RSK <how-to-import-RSK>`, which should be signed by a copy of "
"the :ref:`genuine QMSK <how-to-import-QMSK>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:406
#: d0ebc2876cb94d92ae0018446c9ccee2
msgid "How to verify signatures on Git repository tags and commits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:416
#: bb2e2ec2ed4042c09de1691806c95a1b
msgid ""
":doc:`Import and authenticate keys from the Qubes security pack (qubes-"
"secpack). </project-security/security-pack>` Please see our :ref:`PGP key "
"policies <project-security/security-pack:pgp key policies>` for important "
"information about these keys."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:420
#: ae6adb6265144b87a56440cd588850a4
msgid ""
"Whenever you use one of the `Qubes repositories <https://github.com/"
"QubesOS>`__, you should use Git to verify the PGP signature in a tag on the "
"latest commit or on the latest commit itself. (One or both may be present, "
"but only one is required.) If there is no trusted signed tag or commit on "
"top, any commits after the latest trusted signed tag or commit should "
"**not** be trusted. If you come across a repo with any unsigned commits, you "
"should not add any of your own signed tags or commits on top of them unless "
"you personally vouch for the trustworthiness of the unsigned commits. "
"Instead, ask the person who pushed the unsigned commits to sign them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:422
#: ee25e13e87a24de58f068be3a40b8147
msgid ""
"You should always perform this verification on a trusted local machine with "
"properly authenticated keys rather than relying on a third party, such as "
"GitHub. While the GitHub interface may claim that a commit has a verified "
"signature from a member of the Qubes team, this is only trustworthy if "
"GitHub has performed the signature check correctly, the account identity is "
"authentic, an admin has not replaced the user’s key, GitHub’s servers have "
"not been compromised, and so on. Since there’s no way for you to be certain "
"that all such conditions hold, you’re much better off verifying signatures "
"yourself. (Also see: :ref:`distrusting the infrastructure <introduction/faq:"
"what does it mean to \"distrust the infrastructure\"?>`.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../project-security/verifying-signatures.rst:425
#: 386fc786944b43f5939d8646b0bafa1e
msgid "How to verify a signature on a Git tag"
msgstr ""

